Good afternoon everyone, we will go ahead
and get started.

00:00:09.059 --> 00:00:14.040
On the screen you can see details about what
we will be doing today.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:20.140
This is an update will webinar that we did
a few weeks ago about API keys, for getting

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.439
better data from Eutilities and EDirect.

00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:30.119
If there are questions that we do not get
to answer when I am done talking, at the end

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:36.559
we will be certain to post answers to those
a few days after the webinar is over also.

00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:41.720
The materials, the PowerPoint that I will
be showing along with a PDF version of the

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:49.600
PowerPoint, are available at the go USA.gov
link that you can access any time (go.usa.gov/xnBHR).

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:50.600
Including right now.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.809
Without further ado we will began the webinar
today.

00:00:54.809 --> 00:01:00.999
This is an update, this is a part two of a
discussion that we started a few weeks ago

00:01:00.999 --> 00:01:05.480
on November 8 about API keys for the Eutilities.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:09.590
This is our main API to the Entrez databases.

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:19.280
Also, EDirect, which is a UNIX command line
version is also affected by these new keys.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:25.409
This is essentially an example of community
involvements with webinars which is exciting.

00:01:25.409 --> 00:01:31.500
We had a lot of questions, from our November
8 webinar, we learned a lot of different things.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:36.729
One was there's a lot of community interest
which is great, it was great to hear people

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:42.950
paying attention to this, and also that there
are a number of interesting side topics to

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:48.539
the API keys that we had not included in the
November 8 webinar that people were interested

00:01:48.539 --> 00:01:49.539
in.

00:01:49.539 --> 00:01:51.500
We've included some of that today.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:57.229
It was also clear to us that we needed to
be more clear about a few aspects of how these

00:01:57.229 --> 00:02:04.350
keys will work in particular, I will provide
you with more details today, about exactly

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:10.700
how they work, and some scenarios that might
occur out there, when you are beginning to

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:11.910
set these up.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:17.170
And what some likely consequences of the various
scenarios are.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:22.020
I probably will not cover everything that
might be in everyone's heads out there so

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.800
if you have questions, please let us know.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.790
We're happy to answer those as we can.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:33.330
For any of you who were not at the earlier
webinar on the eighth, I will go through some

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.260
basic things to review what is going on.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:40.470
A quick review of what the Eutilities are
so we know what the API's that will be affected

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:41.470
by this.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:45.630
A little bit about what the keys are, and
then I will expand the discussion about what

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:49.220
happens without a key, and what happens with
the key.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:54.610
And for fun what happens when you mix, when
you have a key and not a key and what kind

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:57.580
of things can happen in those situations.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:03.140
Finally to finish up how do you get the key,
how do you use it, and when does all of this

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:07.000
take effect and when do I have to have things
done by.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:13.250
What we're doing we're introducing new API
keys for the e-utilities API.

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:21.720
Keys are popular with other API providers,
we are looking at these as a way to register

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:27.530
activity and also trying to manage the activity
of these APIs.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:33.810
Pit APIs are free and open to the public,
but with absolutely no regulation in how they

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:37.310
are used they are vulnerable to attack.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:42.650
And service problems because of the high usage
peaks.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:50.400
We want to make this a fair use resource for
everyone, and so on May 1, 2018, we will begin

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:56.490
limiting access to the utilities on the basis
of an IP address.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:58.490
To a rate of no more than three requests per
second.

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:02.610
I will have much more to say about that in
a few slides.

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:03.910
That's for IP's.

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:08.580
So one of the key takeaways is that if you
do not have a key what we will do is look

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:10.430
at the IP address.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:14.760
If you do have a key then you get a higher
rates limit.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:21.079
10 requests per second by default, regardless
of what IP you are using.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:24.300
We only look at the key if the key is present.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:29.919
Then the IP does not matter, the traffic could
be coming for multiple IP's and it is the

00:04:29.919 --> 00:04:34.680
same key and the same regulation, 10 per second
by default.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:40.580
We can negotiate higher rates limits than
10 if you would like and if you can demonstrate

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:41.580
a need.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:45.740
If you are in that situation and you know
that you will be needing more than 10 requests

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:48.500
per second, then please contact us.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:52.139
I will have more information on how to do
that at the end of the webinar.

00:04:52.139 --> 00:04:56.520
That is some basics, what are the e-utilities?

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:00.139
The important parts of the slide I'm sure
that many of you will are familiar with Eutilities,

00:05:00.139 --> 00:05:02.660
but it's important to talk about scope.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:08.370
These keys are only applied to the classic
e-utilities, esearch, esummary, efetch, efetch

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:09.490
and elink.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:12.360
Also espell and egquery.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:22.050
Not to the BLAST API or any other APIs that
are offered, only to these Eutilities for

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:23.900
this point in time.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:29.090
That might change, as this matures but for
now the only thing that we're talking about

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:33.449
are the classic E utilities.

00:05:33.449 --> 00:05:39.490
The key itself, is a string, it is a unique
string the you can acquire it identifies you

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:41.539
to our servers.

00:05:41.539 --> 00:05:45.100
It should be included every time you make
an API request.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:50.790
It is simply a URL parameter, I will talk
about that in a moment, it gets included with

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:54.659
every request and it is attached to a NCBI
account.

00:05:54.659 --> 00:06:02.270
Many of you may be familiar with My NCBI accounts
or submission accounts, they're all the same,

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:04.770
they are all NCBI accounts.

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:10.379
If you have an account you can create a key,
today, and it will be a atached to that account,

00:06:10.379 --> 00:06:14.469
if you do not have an account it is very easy
to make one and then you can create a key

00:06:14.469 --> 00:06:17.800
and it will be attached to that account.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:22.940
Let's talk a little bit about what happens,
what will happen in May if you do not have

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:24.050
a key.

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:26.460
You just go on the way the you have been going.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:32.949
What we will be doing is we will be looking
at the IP addresses from whence the requests

00:06:32.949 --> 00:06:34.180
come.

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:39.150
So I have on the left three users all at different
IP addresses.

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:43.770
So what will happen to them on May 1, when
the switch gets thrown and the API keys are

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:45.449
now in affect.

00:06:45.449 --> 00:06:49.840
This guy here is coming in about two request
per second without a key.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:51.800
That is no problem.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:53.340
He will not notice anything.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:56.870
As long as he does not go about that there
is no problem.

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:02.110
This person is coming through from a different
IP at five request the seconds, what will

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:04.349
happen is they will receive an error message.

00:07:04.349 --> 00:07:10.099
I will show you an example of what those messages
look like, those requests for all intents

00:07:10.099 --> 00:07:13.849
and purposes will fail.

00:07:13.849 --> 00:07:19.979
That person will need to reduce the rate below
3 per second and then he can to resume activity,

00:07:19.979 --> 00:07:26.740
and one thing to be clear, this is not a block
in the sense that NCBI sometimes blocks IPs

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:30.550
because of abuse, and then they have to be
unblocked.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:34.879
Every second we're looking and checking what
the rate is, so all that you have to do is

00:07:34.879 --> 00:07:37.490
reduce the rate and then you will have access.

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:40.840
It is a dynamic rate control.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:45.770
If you are above the three per second limit
you are going to essentially be temporarily

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:47.259
blocked and receive an error message.

00:07:47.259 --> 00:07:51.150
That is the unfortunate fate of the person
in the middle.

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:56.529
The person on the bottom is averaging 1 1/2
request per second there is no problem.

00:07:56.529 --> 00:08:00.439
They can continue on as they have been.

00:08:00.439 --> 00:08:06.039
Let's talk about a slightly different scenario,
Let's talk about two users who are sharing

00:08:06.039 --> 00:08:11.729
the same IP, they may be behind a firewall,
they may be using the same type of software

00:08:11.729 --> 00:08:16.850
interface, whatever it is they are coming
from the same IP address.

00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:22.520
The guy that was doing five request per second
is still out of luck, we see that IP address,

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:24.539
he is not getting through.

00:08:24.539 --> 00:08:31.249
However and this is important, the first user
who is now at the same IP as the second guy,

00:08:31.249 --> 00:08:36.510
is also getting blocked, because that IP is
exceeding the limit.

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:41.000
In fact it is exceeding it by more now because
that IP is now giving a seven requests per

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.430
second.

00:08:42.430 --> 00:08:45.090
So both users are blocked.

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:52.220
So while this person was not actually exceeding
the limits on his or her own, they give blocked

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:56.980
because someone else is using the same IP
and they are exceeding the limit.

00:08:56.980 --> 00:09:00.910
Or I could of made a lot of slides here, I'm
try to keep this brief, let's say there are

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:05.420
several people there all doing one request
per second all of the same time, if there

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:08.780
are five of them that is five request for
second and they all give blocked.

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:12.770
It is the IP address.

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:17.160
However that works for you, the IP address
is what we're looking at if the key is not

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:18.160
present.

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:23.810
Here's an IP address down here, only one person
is using this address, they are still doing

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:26.800
one a half request per second and there is
no problem.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:30.380
The IP address has not exceeded the rate.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:36.850
That is the important thing to remember it
is about IP addresses, come May 1st that is

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:37.850
how we will look at it.

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:38.850
Had you measure the rates?

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:41.050
There have been several questions about this.

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:46.960
What I've done is to put a time bar at the
top, each one of these weird little H like

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:48.910
things, is a request.

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:54.330
Request start, execution and request stop
when the data is delivered back to you.

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:57.830
These are the execution times you can imagine
on our servers.

00:09:57.830 --> 00:10:01.390
I am not including Internet traffic and all
of that.

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:04.120
This is what the NCBI servers would see.

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:09.080
Your request comes in at a particular time,
it executes for a particular time and then

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:10.500
it finishes.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:16.600
The rates the we're looking at are the rates
that requests are received by our servers.

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:24.290
Their time stamped at the request start, it
does not matter how long the request takes

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:30.600
to execute, it is only when we see the initial
request, so let's take this first case.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:37.070
This could be a single IP address, or it could
be a key, it is an activity stream that we

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:38.440
are seeing.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:44.590
There is no case where more than three of
these request starts occur within the same

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:45.590
second.

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:47.630
That is what is important.

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:53.350
It is a moving window, not that we count every
second but it is a moving second window, if

00:10:53.350 --> 00:10:59.040
you look at the second what there will be
a problem here because there is one 2, 3,

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:03.210
4 requests the begin in that second window.

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:05.740
So that will throw the error.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:08.320
That is when the rate is exceeded.

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:14.000
Just to prove another point, here is a case
where the requests are taking a long time,

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:20.260
these requests are taking a few hundred milliseconds,
these requests might be taking over one second,

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:24.960
certainly the case with efetch or elink the
request can take many seconds.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:28.210
So what happens in that case?

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:33.260
This is all fine, this person will not receive
any errors because there is no case when the

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:37.450
request starts are more than three per second.

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:42.140
It is absolutely true that during this period
there are four concurrent requests that our

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:48.010
servers are executing from the same IP address,
or the same key, that is not what we're counting,

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:53.640
we're not counting simultaneous execution
we are looking at the request start times.

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:58.070
You can have as many of these things going
at the same time as you want as long as you

00:11:58.070 --> 00:12:01.270
do not start requests more than three times
a second.

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:05.900
I hope that that is clear that is what we're
doing in terms of the rates.

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:10.250
Now that we understand what is happening,
if you do not have a key and how we calculate

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:11.440
the rates.

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:13.300
What happens if you have a key?

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:18.880
Okay so I have my key, I'm going to start
using it.

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:21.130
After May 1st what will be expected happened?

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:27.750
Here is the first guy with the IP address,
now I can do up to 10, I'm doing seven requests

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:30.410
per second here is my key.

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:35.641
Notice that api underscore key is the parameter
that I use, that is what you put in the utility

00:12:35.641 --> 00:12:39.650
requests in the URL and here is my key.

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:43.230
Yours will be significantly longer than that
but just to fit it on the slide.

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:45.870
This is your little key.

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:50.450
They can go on as much as they want at seven
requests per second.

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:56.720
Here is another guy with 15 requests per second,
we told you that 10 requests per second is

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:59.140
a limit so you get an error message.

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:05.260
Just like someone without a key over three,
the same error messages.

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:07.410
You are temporarily blocked.

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:14.320
What this person would need to do with their
requests, is calm that down a bit below 10,

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:17.480
and then they will have access restored again.

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:18.480
Immediately.

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:22.980
Here's another guy, he is doing four requests
per second and that is great, he has his own

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:26.030
key, all is hunky-dory.

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:29.700
So let's think about some other situations.

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:32.680
What if these people share the same IP?

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:37.600
That is what we were talking about before,
these users are behind the firewall, at the

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:43.100
same company, whatever it might be, they are
coming to NCBI with the same IP.

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:45.930
It does not matter anymore.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:47.910
Because they have separate keys.

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:50.670
The only thing that we're looking at is the
key.

00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:55.820
So this key has seven requests per second
and it is totally fine and he goes through.

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:59.680
This key has 15 and that exceeds the rate.

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:01.190
It gets blocked.

00:14:01.190 --> 00:14:07.340
Previously when people from the same IP would
have both gotten blocked by one person at

00:14:07.340 --> 00:14:11.570
the IP exceeding the rate, or if they collectively
were exceeded the rate.

00:14:11.570 --> 00:14:16.120
If everybody has a key you are independent.

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:21.000
You are looking at the individual keys and
it does not matter how many keys are behind

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.640
the same IP it is key by key.

00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:31.000
Again this guy is not affected, he still does
has one IP, he has is key, he is under 10

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:33.890
per second he is good.

00:14:33.890 --> 00:14:41.280
Let's look at the case, when someone is sharing
a key.

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:47.860
Now all the IPs are different but the first
two cases are sharing the same key.

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:53.070
Maybe you decided that different users of
your software share the same key.

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:56.780
Or you have shared the key with a colleague,
what happens?

00:14:56.780 --> 00:15:03.180
This guy thinks he is great because he's doing
seven per second and everything great.

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:10.850
But down the hall or wherever, someone else
is using the same key at 15 so they are both

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:11.850
blocked.

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:18.500
It is the key that is seeing 22 requests per
second, that is way over 10, everything with

00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:24.060
that key will be blocked until that rates
gets below 10.

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:28.840
That is the key thing to remember once you
have keys if you share them you become vulnerable

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:34.620
with any person that has the key to block
everybody with the key.

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:39.930
And this person is still fine, he his own
key and is cruising at four per second no

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:41.300
problem.

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:43.620
Let's think of another scenario.

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:45.320
Let's think of a big organization.

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:48.220
Everyone has the same IP.

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:58.510
We're all behind the same firewall or gateway,
two of us have a key and this guy does not.

00:15:58.510 --> 00:15:59.510
So what is happening?

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:05.060
I have my own key, I told you it would be
better if you have your individual key, I'm

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:09.420
cruising in a seven per second, great no peoblem.

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:13.430
Because your key is unique, and no one else
is using it and you are beneath ten requests

00:16:13.430 --> 00:16:14.790
per second.

00:16:14.790 --> 00:16:19.970
This guy though, has a key and he is still
insisting at 15 requests per second it will

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:23.370
not work, he gets blocked.

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:29.410
He gets error messages, because his key is
generating more than 10 requests per second.

00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:30.410
Regardless of the IP.

00:16:30.410 --> 00:16:36.800
However here is this guy, some guy down the
hall on a different floor maybe over in Europe

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:41.490
somewhere did not get the message, didnt get
the memo about the API keys, is not using

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:42.490
one.

00:16:42.490 --> 00:16:44.940
He has no key, what happens.

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:51.050
IF it has no key, then we look at the IP address,
we look at the IP address and say what is

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:53.620
coming from that IP address?

00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:58.250
Goodness there is 24 requests per second coming
from this IP address from all three of these

00:16:58.250 --> 00:17:00.390
people, blocked.

00:17:00.390 --> 00:17:04.189
It exceeds three requests per second.

00:17:04.189 --> 00:17:05.750
So this is a problem.

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:11.069
You have some people sharing and IP address,
some do not have keys the people without keys

00:17:11.069 --> 00:17:16.760
will be affected by the people who do not
keys at a higher rate.

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:22.589
So that is something to be aware of, for these
are cases where you are sharing an IP address.

00:17:22.589 --> 00:17:24.299
How would this not of been as bad?

00:17:24.299 --> 00:17:28.779
If he had a different IP address this would
be solved.

00:17:28.779 --> 00:17:33.250
Because then we would look at his IP or her
IP and we would only be seeing to requests

00:17:33.250 --> 00:17:35.169
per second from that IP.

00:17:35.169 --> 00:17:38.610
These people's activity would not be on the
same IP address.

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:41.140
So we would not notice it.

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:46.230
It is only the case if you do not have the
key.

00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:48.970
To review, these are the principles.

00:17:48.970 --> 00:17:53.529
Any IP that posts more than three requests
per second will receive errors if the key

00:17:53.529 --> 00:17:55.309
is not present.

00:17:55.309 --> 00:17:58.870
If the key is not present we look at the IP
address.

00:17:58.870 --> 00:18:00.879
And three is the maximum.

00:18:00.879 --> 00:18:05.980
If the key is present, we ignore the IP address
and we look at any activity that is coming

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:07.780
from that key.

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:11.950
Some people ask, well let's say that I have
my laptop at home and my computer at work,

00:18:11.950 --> 00:18:16.750
and my computer at my consultant's office
and they're all different things and I'm using

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:19.610
the same key everywhere?

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:25.840
All of those activity streams sum, from our
point of view into one activity from the same

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:26.840
key.

00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:30.019
If the sum of all that activity is more than
10 there will be a problem.

00:18:30.019 --> 00:18:31.669
You will get error messages.

00:18:31.669 --> 00:18:36.960
When you are thinking about your actual activity
you want to think about these principles and

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:41.620
think about how many keys are you going to
share, if you will share them at all, it is

00:18:41.620 --> 00:18:42.620
risky right?

00:18:42.620 --> 00:18:46.649
If you share a key anyone the goes above the
limit will cause problems for everyone with

00:18:46.649 --> 00:18:48.139
the key.

00:18:48.139 --> 00:18:52.870
You want to think about what the limits are
and what they might be, again if ten is not

00:18:52.870 --> 00:18:58.390
going to work for you, and we understand that,
we are perfectly happy to negotiate a higher

00:18:58.390 --> 00:18:59.830
rate if you need that.

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:05.289
We need you to work with us to demonstrate
why you need it and we can help you get to

00:19:05.289 --> 00:19:07.809
a rate that will work for you.

00:19:07.809 --> 00:19:10.340
All right.

00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:13.490
So a few more details that came out of some
of the questions.

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:15.610
We will cover these quickly.

00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:17.690
There may be more that you are curious about.

00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:20.840
This is the error message, it will look like
this.

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:26.039
We're thinking about providing a service in
the near future where you can actually send

00:19:26.039 --> 00:19:30.559
a request and get the error message, it is
not live yet but we are considering doing

00:19:30.559 --> 00:19:34.240
something like that because we understand
that many people would like to have their

00:19:34.240 --> 00:19:39.529
software get it directly and then parse it
or detect it in some way.

00:19:39.529 --> 00:19:43.129
This is essentially what this will look like.

00:19:43.129 --> 00:19:47.070
If the request produces an error it is like
a bounce.

00:19:47.070 --> 00:19:50.269
The request does not execute.

00:19:50.269 --> 00:19:52.320
So you would need to resubmit that request.

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:58.580
It is not a delay it is not a time of execution
penalty at this point it just bounces off.

00:19:58.580 --> 00:20:00.330
It is dead.

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:04.679
You will need to resubmit any request that
received an error message.

00:20:04.679 --> 00:20:08.620
At least you get the error message back so
that you know which requests received that

00:20:08.620 --> 00:20:13.779
message, any request that did not receive
the measures is fine, once you get the error

00:20:13.779 --> 00:20:15.539
you will need to resubmit.

00:20:15.539 --> 00:20:19.379
A few questions about encryption.

00:20:19.379 --> 00:20:26.350
All Eutility traffic now should be coming
over HTTPS, which encrypts all of the parameters

00:20:26.350 --> 00:20:33.140
in the URL including the key, but if you are
passing the key around any other way before

00:20:33.140 --> 00:20:40.850
you get it into the request object, that encryption
is up to you, the developer.

00:20:40.850 --> 00:20:47.399
All we're saying, is HTTPS will take care
of the actual communication request, and the

00:20:47.399 --> 00:20:53.990
encryption of that request, anything else
to manipulate the key before or after the

00:20:53.990 --> 00:20:59.740
request, that is a to you in terms of how
you want to think about security.

00:20:59.740 --> 00:21:01.590
And just to make the point again.

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:06.350
The only way to prevent multiple users from
interfering with each other is for them each

00:21:06.350 --> 00:21:07.350
to have their own key.

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:08.909
That is the ideal.

00:21:08.909 --> 00:21:13.679
It is their own fault if they go over the
limit.

00:21:13.679 --> 00:21:17.889
Once you start sharing, and I'm not saying
that you should not share there may be reasonable

00:21:17.889 --> 00:21:23.779
cases where sharing is reasonable, but just
be aware that you could have people surprised

00:21:23.779 --> 00:21:29.309
and getting affected by other users that they
have no idea about.

00:21:29.309 --> 00:21:33.620
So we talked about this last time, just in
case you have not heard, all you have to do

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:35.690
to create a key is to get a NCBI account.

00:21:35.690 --> 00:21:42.520
You can find the account login in the upper
right-hand side of any page very easy to create

00:21:42.520 --> 00:21:43.710
an account.

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:48.480
You will find on the main page of the account,
there is an API key management section and

00:21:48.480 --> 00:21:49.480
click the button.

00:21:49.480 --> 00:21:52.549
You click the button get the key copy it and
use it.

00:21:52.549 --> 00:21:54.510
That is it.

00:21:54.510 --> 00:22:00.039
One key per NCBI account.

00:22:00.039 --> 00:22:05.249
If you need additional keys you will need
to make additional accounts to have those

00:22:05.249 --> 00:22:07.570
keys.

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:12.289
Again I have alluded to this, all you do to
use the key is place the value and assign

00:22:12.289 --> 00:22:18.759
it to this parameter, api underscore key,
in any Eutility request.

00:22:18.759 --> 00:22:25.149
If you are using edirect, there is an even
simpler environmental variable, NCBI underscore

00:22:25.149 --> 00:22:31.669
API underscore KEY, you just set that environmental
variable to your key and you are done.

00:22:31.669 --> 00:22:37.179
You can do that in a config file or however
you would like to do that.

00:22:37.179 --> 00:22:42.559
Once the environmental variable is set, Edirect
will look at that, and provide the key to

00:22:42.559 --> 00:22:44.700
all the requests automatically.

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:50.890
A question about this, if you do not like
your key anymore, you think you got stolen,

00:22:50.890 --> 00:22:58.580
you are concerned about the key, you can always
get rid of it, go back to your NCBI account,

00:22:58.580 --> 00:23:04.850
create a key, and then the old one will immediately
be defunct, and the new one will be immediately

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:05.850
activated.

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:10.091
You can only have one key per account, you
can replace at any time, just remember when

00:23:10.091 --> 00:23:14.490
you replace it the other one is immediately
dead.

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:19.320
This all happens on May first of next year.

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:24.090
We would encourage you to go ahead and get
a key, start playing with that, it is not

00:23:24.090 --> 00:23:25.880
going to do anything.

00:23:25.880 --> 00:23:27.940
There are no restrictions in effect currently.

00:23:27.940 --> 00:23:32.710
We was still encourage you to get a key is
start thinking about how you would like to

00:23:32.710 --> 00:23:33.710
strategize.

00:23:33.710 --> 00:23:40.840
If you are going to share it, provide users,
ask for users to create a key, whatever strategy

00:23:40.840 --> 00:23:45.529
you want to think about, please start thinking
about it now.

00:23:45.529 --> 00:23:52.890
We would also encourage you as developers,
to continue using the tool and email parameters

00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:55.269
they are very helpful.

00:23:55.269 --> 00:23:59.539
The tool really should be the value, the value
of the parameter should be the name of the

00:23:59.539 --> 00:24:06.450
software package, you can potentially have
many keys associated with that one tool value.

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:11.400
The key does not necessarily help us understand
what the software package is, and that can

00:24:11.400 --> 00:24:16.950
really help us help you in terms of monitoring
activity and getting back to you about concerns

00:24:16.950 --> 00:24:18.950
with what is going on with the activity.

00:24:18.950 --> 00:24:23.130
If someone is abusing your tool we can help
you understand how that is happening.

00:24:23.130 --> 00:24:28.169
We cannot do that just from the key we need
the tool value to help you ideally.

00:24:28.169 --> 00:24:33.899
The email is very helpful to be a developer
contact because if you are distributing something

00:24:33.899 --> 00:24:39.230
the end-users will have keys but we do not
need their email addresses we need your email

00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:44.380
address as a developer as to who to talk to
about how to solve the problem with the software.

00:24:44.380 --> 00:24:50.760
So we continue to encourage you to provide
us with those contacts, so that we can help

00:24:50.760 --> 00:24:53.890
you in case there are issues.

00:24:53.890 --> 00:24:59.830
The tool and email parameters are not required,
have nothing to do the keys, but we would

00:24:59.830 --> 00:25:05.590
encourage you to continue using them to allow
us to have that direct contact.

00:25:05.590 --> 00:25:10.950
One thing I want to announce, in early 2018
we will be conducting a few test periods when

00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:13.460
the API keys turn on.

00:25:13.460 --> 00:25:16.970
This has been a request from several people,
they would like to test them.

00:25:16.970 --> 00:25:20.399
They would like to test their code with the
keys working.

00:25:20.399 --> 00:25:24.929
We do not have dates or times planned yet
but we will not be doing these until the earliest

00:25:24.929 --> 00:25:32.190
January 2018, very likely for a few hours
on a particular day we will turn them on,

00:25:32.190 --> 00:25:37.679
and then you can have at it and test your
code and see if the keys are working as you

00:25:37.679 --> 00:25:38.679
would expect them to.

00:25:38.679 --> 00:25:42.350
We obviously are very interested in how that
works out for you, because it is a test for

00:25:42.350 --> 00:25:43.779
us to.

00:25:43.779 --> 00:25:49.270
So that will be happening at least a few times
throughout early 2018.

00:25:49.270 --> 00:25:50.809
Well before the May 1st date.

00:25:50.809 --> 00:25:58.120
We will be publicizing those on the blog and
social media and on the E utilities announcement.

00:25:58.120 --> 00:26:04.270
There is a great email list, I encourage any
of you would like to keep abreast of those

00:26:04.270 --> 00:26:06.970
things, to subscribe to that utilities-announce.

00:26:06.970 --> 00:26:12.909
We will very likely be doing more webinars
more posts like this as the date approaches.

00:26:12.909 --> 00:26:16.639
If you have additional questions about that
please let us know.

00:26:16.639 --> 00:26:20.369
Write to us, info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov if you
have any questions after this webinar.

00:26:20.369 --> 00:26:24.269
I think at this point I will be quiet and
see if we have any questions.

00:26:24.269 --> 00:26:28.600
There were a couple of questions during the
talk.

00:26:28.600 --> 00:26:36.559
They circulate around the idea of everyone
having his or her own key.

00:26:36.559 --> 00:26:42.340
I want to emphasize that, if you are distributing
software you should have some mechanism for

00:26:42.340 --> 00:26:48.539
your end user to enter the key that they have
obtained themselves from NCBI.

00:26:48.539 --> 00:26:52.580
Someone suggested that we put that in the
documentation, I think we should do that.

00:26:52.580 --> 00:26:57.879
If it is not in the utilities documentation
I think we should add that.

00:26:57.879 --> 00:27:03.700
Right now that is it, I think you have answered
the other question that this person had about

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:09.289
the IP versus what happens when you have a
key I think you covered that fairly thoroughly.

00:27:09.289 --> 00:27:13.029
If you have additional questions we will stay
open a couple more minutes and we can answer

00:27:13.029 --> 00:27:14.029
them.

00:27:14.029 --> 00:27:18.110
I see another question.

00:27:18.110 --> 00:27:22.660
Does this change the overnight window for
large jobs?

00:27:22.660 --> 00:27:25.400
So the answer to that is essentially no.

00:27:25.400 --> 00:27:31.850
What we have been doing in the past, when
we have not had keys, it has been more unregulated,

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:38.090
we have encouraged people if you have large
jobs to do those in the overnight hours or

00:27:38.090 --> 00:27:42.820
on the weekend simply because you are likely
to get better response times, and you are

00:27:42.820 --> 00:27:45.830
likely to not interfere with other people
as much.

00:27:45.830 --> 00:27:47.730
When you are doing that.

00:27:47.730 --> 00:27:52.409
I think that advice would still hold going
forward.

00:27:52.409 --> 00:27:58.190
What we would also anticipate is that
once the keys go live you should have less

00:27:58.190 --> 00:28:06.809
cases of high volume traffic that would impede
you because the keys will be limiting that.

00:28:06.809 --> 00:28:12.690
It will not be as much of a problem as to
when you actually submit the jobs, the keys

00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:17.869
are active 24 seven, it does not matter when
the request come in, they will be limited

00:28:17.869 --> 00:28:20.480
the same way regardless of the time.

00:28:20.480 --> 00:28:26.970
It is likely, the way the traffic patterns
work you will probably get better service

00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:31.549
to certain extent more freedom on the servers
overnight and on the weekends.

00:28:31.549 --> 00:28:36.359
There is no reason for you to change that
behavior.

00:28:36.359 --> 00:28:38.059
A couple of more questions.

00:28:38.059 --> 00:28:42.070
Will there be a way for us to obtain multiple
keys and provide them to our users?

00:28:42.070 --> 00:28:51.190
That is a great question at this point we
do not have a mechanism in place for a particular

00:28:51.190 --> 00:28:54.980
automatic call to generate multiple keys.

00:28:54.980 --> 00:29:01.889
It is something that we have noted from one
or two people, it is on the list of considerations.

00:29:01.889 --> 00:29:09.239
For right now, the only way to get a key is
to establish a NCBI account, and then get

00:29:09.239 --> 00:29:10.860
the key that way.

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:15.679
So, if we have an update on out we will certainly
let you know, it has been something that a

00:29:15.679 --> 00:29:21.169
few people have asked about, but at the moment
there is no automatic way to get a key.

00:29:21.169 --> 00:29:25.220
Another one, any suggestions for server-based
software?

00:29:25.220 --> 00:29:28.389
For our software there is not a good user
to use for a key.

00:29:28.389 --> 00:29:37.710
So I suppose for that, it might be interesting
to hear more about your specific situation.

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:39.940
In that case.

00:29:39.940 --> 00:29:47.299
There have been, one thing I guess I should
say, it is important to know what your frequency

00:29:47.299 --> 00:29:48.299
of load is.

00:29:48.299 --> 00:29:50.509
To have some understanding of that.

00:29:50.509 --> 00:29:57.059
It may be that in many cases the three per
second limit will be rare for you to hit from

00:29:57.059 --> 00:29:58.059
a particular IP.

00:29:58.059 --> 00:30:03.129
I do not know how distributed your package
is.

00:30:03.129 --> 00:30:07.789
Or how many IP's we would be seeing from the
activity from your package.

00:30:07.789 --> 00:30:14.220
If it is really just one IP, then you need
to be much more concerned about the rate obviously.

00:30:14.220 --> 00:30:20.639
If it is a distributed thing, and you have
thousands of users, all over the place, then

00:30:20.639 --> 00:30:26.850
each user would have to exceed that 3 per
second rate on their own to have an effect

00:30:26.850 --> 00:30:28.100
at all.

00:30:28.100 --> 00:30:33.539
so that's one question, you may not need the
keys.

00:30:33.539 --> 00:30:37.940
Otherwise it is something where each of the
individual distributed packages would need

00:30:37.940 --> 00:30:45.109
its own key in some way to be able to get
over those limits or to have the limits be

00:30:45.109 --> 00:30:46.109
higher.

00:30:46.109 --> 00:30:50.619
I'm not sure I'm answering your question well
if I am not I would be happy to follow up

00:30:50.619 --> 00:30:57.159
with you after this with the more particulars
about your specific situation.

00:30:57.159 --> 00:31:01.639
Another question, do we have documentation
on how to put the key in the code?

00:31:01.639 --> 00:31:07.149
It simply goes in the URL with however you
were passing the URL to us.

00:31:07.149 --> 00:31:08.149
Right.

00:31:08.149 --> 00:31:10.289
Thanks for the question.

00:31:10.289 --> 00:31:16.399
We have not put anything in the documentation
specifically about this, we can put some guidance

00:31:16.399 --> 00:31:22.989
in, as Peter was saying, the thing that is
necessary is for the key to go into the URL.

00:31:22.989 --> 00:31:24.549
Or the post.

00:31:24.549 --> 00:31:27.879
Whatever object you are creating to send the
request.

00:31:27.879 --> 00:31:29.269
It needs to go in there.

00:31:29.269 --> 00:31:31.440
How it goes in there we do not care.

00:31:31.440 --> 00:31:35.729
There is any number of ways they can be put
in there.

00:31:35.729 --> 00:31:42.470
Like what E direct does, this is one straightforward
way to do this, the key get stored in a configuration

00:31:42.470 --> 00:31:47.440
entity, it is an environmental variable, it
could be a configuration file, or anything

00:31:47.440 --> 00:31:54.190
else that is stored in the software as a preference,
on the disc as a config, whatever it might

00:31:54.190 --> 00:31:58.610
be, the key is stored somewhere the software
reads it and puts in the requests.

00:31:58.610 --> 00:32:01.309
That is one approach.

00:32:01.309 --> 00:32:05.519
I'm sure there are many approaches that I
will not think about or think of because there

00:32:05.519 --> 00:32:10.830
are many creative people out there in terms
of how you deal with keys.

00:32:10.830 --> 00:32:14.470
Effectively there is any way that you would
like to do this is fine as long as the key

00:32:14.470 --> 00:32:16.769
gets into the request.

00:32:16.769 --> 00:32:25.249
We may be updating the documentation to add
examples, of how to do that, but I think that

00:32:25.249 --> 00:32:28.159
is all that I can really say right now.

00:32:28.159 --> 00:32:31.489
Do you think that it would be a good idea
for the person who asked about the server

00:32:31.489 --> 00:32:33.009
software, should they write to you?

00:32:33.009 --> 00:32:35.379
Yes absolutely wright to me.

00:32:35.379 --> 00:32:45.419
It's sayers@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov which is 
the same suffix that you see, you can also

00:32:45.419 --> 00:32:50.390
just write to info and it will get to me or
someone who can answer your question,

00:32:50.390 --> 00:32:51.510
no problem.

00:32:51.510 --> 00:32:54.539
We would love to talk to about that.

00:32:54.539 --> 00:32:58.619
Were absolutely confident that there are many
situations we have not thought about as much

00:32:58.619 --> 00:33:04.009
as we need to so if you do have a situation
that we are not covering, please let us know.

00:33:04.009 --> 00:33:08.000
I'm sure there are other people in your situation
as well.

00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:10.400
Thank you Eric thank you everybody for coming today.

00:33:10.400 --> 00:33:12.500
I think we will and the webinar at this point.

